WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.190
(energetic acoustic music)

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:03.720
- Across the whole foods system,

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.110
20% of fruits and
vegetables that are grown

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:08.440
never make it to a human mouth.

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.960
Food waste is a complex
problem, some of it is

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.410
because of the really
tight aesthetic standards

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:16.120
of the grocery stores,
need to be perfectly round,

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:17.760
the same size, the same color.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.960
My name is Ben Chesler, and
I'm one of the co-founders

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.173
of Imperfect Foods.

00:00:22.023 --> 00:00:25.470
(groovy acoustic music)

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.500
Imperfect Foods is a
grocery delivery service

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:28.950
that takes produce and groceries

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.780
that would normally go to
waste and delivers them

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.140
to your door for 30% less
than the grocery store.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:34.530
We started this because

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:36.400
we noticed there was
an environmental need,

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:37.750
and there was food going to waste.

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:39.920
If food waste were a
country, it would actually be

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.340
the third largest emitter
of green house gases,

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.640
and then there's all of the
resources, all the water,

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:47.220
all the labor, that goes to waste.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:48.710
We know the more people we reach,

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:50.980
the bigger impact we're able
to have on the environment.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:52.540
We wanted to be close to the farmers

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:53.830
and close to the source,

00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:56.450
and over half of the
produce consumed in the US

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:59.410
is grown in California,
and San Francisco it was.

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:02.904
We came out here with basically
a forklift, a warehouse,

00:01:02.904 --> 00:01:04.460
and the shirts on our backs.

00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:05.670
The way we work with farms,

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:07.290
we're here to help them reduce waste,

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.588
so every week, farms
basically send us a list

00:01:09.588 --> 00:01:13.160
of the fruits and vegetables
that they can't sell,

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:15.200
and we're putting
together a complete menu.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.890
When you talk to the farmers
you really understand

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:20.220
these fruits and vegetables
are kind of their children

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:23.320
and they put so much care
and love into growing them,

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:25.071
and they have so much
pride in their craft.

00:01:25.071 --> 00:01:27.120
(rhythmic acoustic guitar music)

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.670
- I love farming because
I like the challenge,

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:33.010
I like always going up
against Mother Nature.

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:36.490
Lakeside Organic Gardens,
we farm over 3,000 acres,

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:39.410
we grow over 52 different
types of vegetables,

00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:42.500
cilantro, parsley, broccoli,
cauliflower, parsnips,

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:45.120
rutabegas, turnips, lettuces all types,

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.780
romaine, green leaf, red
leaf, iceberg, chard.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.090
We consider ourselves a one stop shop

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:52.483
for organic vegetables.

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:58.130
We're in a red cabbage field
where it's just been harvested.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:01.990
Here on my left, I have
a Lake Side regular box,

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:04.430
and to my right I have the Imperfect box.

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:07.190
All of them are uniform standard, pretty.

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:10.010
This is just exactly the
same, same field, same deal,

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:11.353
just a little bit smaller.

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:14.550
Ideally, for any farmer in this world,

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:17.340
the perfect scenario
is harvest every piece

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.920
of produce that's out in the field.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:20.753
Delicious.

00:02:20.753 --> 00:02:23.050
Before we teamed up with Imperfect Foods,

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:25.360
you'd have quite a bit of
product left out in the fields.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:27.840
Now we have an outlet for those vegetables

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.680
that aren't as pretty, but
they taste just the same

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:31.970
as anything else.

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:34.020
Like they say it, "Don't
judge a book by its cover,"

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:35.850
same with vegetables, you know?

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:37.590
If they're a little bit off,

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.280
don't knock it till you try it.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:42.030
(somber banjo music)

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:43.900
- We're here in the
Imperfect Foods warehouse

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:46.467
in San Francisco, and so this
is where all the produce,

00:02:46.467 --> 00:02:49.230
the groceries, the meat,
the dairy, everything,

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:51.670
comes in from the farms,
so let's go take a look

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:53.340
at some of the product.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.300
Why would these mandarins go to Imperfect,

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:57.190
not a grocery store, and you
can see some of this, like,

00:02:57.190 --> 00:02:59.920
external scarring,
roughness of the outside

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:01.790
or discoloration, little bit of peeling.

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:03.550
The grocery stores really don't want them,

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:05.830
and they would normally
go to waste on the farm.

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:07.970
About a year ago we
expanded beyond produce,

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:10.040
because we noticed that
there was food going to waste

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:11.480
across the whole food system.

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:13.770
It's really well aligned
that every box of produce

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:16.620
and groceries we sell helps
us fight climate change

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:18.030
and do some good for the environment.

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.170
One of the main things
that drives us to be better

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:22.880
and keep pushing is knowing
that our work is helping

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.670
to leave the planet in a better place

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:26.370
than we found it for our children.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:29.320
Now we're headed to Food Shift,

00:03:29.320 --> 00:03:31.680
which is one of our community
partners in the Bay Area.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:34.050
So sometimes when we have
product that we can't sell,

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:35.330
we want it to still find a home

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:37.580
so we donate to organizations
like Food Shift.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:41.330
- We are a combination of
food recovery organization

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:43.170
and a social enterprise kitchen.

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:45.560
We educate folks who would otherwise

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:48.800
face high barrier to entry for employment,

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:52.240
and we hire them for our own enterprise,

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:55.380
catering and distribution
of produced meals.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:58.290
I want everybody to be mindful
and be kind to each other,

00:03:58.290 --> 00:03:59.990
and food is a great way to do that,

00:03:59.990 --> 00:04:02.360
'cause when we break our
proverbial bread together,

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:05.480
I think we're closer, we
think about our sameness,

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:08.490
we solve things together, we
put aside our differences,

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:10.500
or we even embrace our differences.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.500
What a privilege that we get to eat,

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:15.113
and what a privilege that we get to cook.

00:04:16.112 --> 00:04:18.510
- It's amazing that food
is really so central

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:21.070
to people's identity,
and it can really serve

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:22.470
as a force to bring people together.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:23.960
It's also one of the biggest issues

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:25.930
affecting climate change,

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:27.760
and in health, and nutrition, obesity.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:30.272
So it is really crazy how this small seed

00:04:30.272 --> 00:04:32.550
turns into a piece of fruit or vegetable

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:35.140
and then basically can have
so many positive effects

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:36.201
on the world.

00:04:36.201 --> 00:04:39.201
(happy synth music)

00:04:41.419 --> 00:04:43.452
(whooshing)

00:04:43.452 --> 00:04:45.702
(creaking)

